// Client reads the token from localStorage and sends it manually.
// Server side simply returns the raw token in the login response body:
@PostMapping("/login")
public Map<String, String> login(@RequestBody LoginRequest req) {
    authenticate(req);
    String jwt = jwtService.generateToken(req.username());
    return Map.of("token", jwt); // client stores this in localStorage -> readable by any JS
}
```
Convenient for SPAs and mobile, but any injected script can read the token.

```java
// Server sets the token in an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict cookie.
@PostMapping("/login")
public ResponseEntity<Void> login(@RequestBody LoginRequest req) {
    authenticate(req);
    String jwt = jwtService.generateToken(req.username());
    ResponseCookie cookie = ResponseCookie.from("token", jwt)
        .httpOnly(true)        // JavaScript cannot read it -> blocks XSS theft
        .secure(true)          // HTTPS only
        .sameSite("Strict")    // blocks CSRF
        .path("/")
        .maxAge(Duration.ofMinutes(15))
        .build();
    return ResponseEntity.noContent().header(HttpHeaders.SET_COOKIE, cookie.toString()).build();
}
```
The browser sends the cookie automatically and JavaScript never touches the token.

