// Stateful: Spring creates an HTTP session and a JSESSIONID cookie.
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth.anyRequest().authenticated())
        .formLogin(Customizer.withDefaults())
        .sessionManagement(s -> s
            .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.IF_REQUIRED)) // server stores state
        .build();
}
```
Server-side session state can be invalidated instantly, but needs sticky sessions or Redis to scale.

```java
// Stateless: no session; a JWT filter rebuilds the SecurityContext per request.
@Bean
SecurityFilterChain chain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth.anyRequest().authenticated())
        .sessionManagement(s -> s
            .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS)) // server stores nothing
        .addFilterBefore(jwtFilter, UsernamePasswordAuthenticationFilter.class)
        .build();
}
```
Any server can validate the token, but you cannot revoke it before it expires.
